.05419921875, -0.023101806640625, 0.0137786865234375, 0.01465606689453125, 0.01226043701171875, -0.032501220703125, -0.01324462890625, 0.0142669677734375, 0.003997802734375, 0.01507568359375, -0.01235198974609375, 0.02838134765625, 0.043792724609375, 0.0292205810546875, 0.0151519775390625, -0.002285003662109375, -0.0262451171875, 0.021148681640625, 0.05517578125, -0.034515380859375, -0.0084228515625, -0.043975830078125, -0.04608154296875, 0.00799560546875, -0.005153656005859375, -0.005283355712890625, -0.00592803955078125, 0.01519012451171875, -0.0158233642578125, 0.0185546875, -0.0007014274597167969, -0.016204833984375, -0.01898193359375, 0.005229949951171875, -0.00147247314453125, 0.01103973388671875, -0.01514434814453125, -0.0037593841552734375, -0.0029239654541015625, 0.0144805908203125, -0.0066375732421875, 0.006687164306640625, 0.0276641845703125, -0.005237579345703125, 0.0184173583984375, -0.024505615234375, -0.004467010498046875, 0.0176544189453125, -0.0064544677734375, 0.0038928985595703125, 0.0005536079406738281], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. Our research addresses the question: Can in-house small language models (SLMs) effectively automate invoice categorisation while maintaining cost efficiency, data security, and interpretability?\n\nWhile artificial intelligence is increasingly used to automate such tasks, we focus on SLMs\u2014specifically, the Sentence-BERT (SBERT) model and DeBERTa (Decoding-enhanced BERT with Disentangled Attention)\u2014which are more manageable for in-house deployment compared to large language models (LLMs). SBERT is a sentence transformer designed to generate semantically meaningful sentence embeddings, while DeBERTa is a transformer-based model known for its strong performance on language understanding tasks.\n\nWe begin by analysing the pre-trained embedding geometry of these models on a financial invoice corpus. Here, \"embedding geometry\" refers to the spatial arrangement of sentence representations in the model\u2019s high-dimensional vector space. We observe that the embedding space is globally anisotropic (i.e., the distribution of embeddings is uneven across directions) but contains locally isotropic clusters (i.e., within certain regions, embeddings are evenly distributed). These clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key driver of semantic similarity in invoice texts. This extends prior findings at the token level to sentence embeddings in a financial context.\n\nOur dataset comprises thousands of invoices from a diverse set of clients and vendors, ensuring a range of invoice types and GL codes. We split the data into training, validation, and test sets to evaluate model performance. SBERT, fine-tuned on a single GPU, achieves 0.96 accuracy and 0.9 F1 score on the test set for invoice classification, outperforming both a zero-shot LLM baseline and a vendor identity-only baseline. Notably, SBERT shows improved performance on smaller, more challenging categories and for new clients, demonstrating promising generalisation. The F1 score was calculated as the harmonic mean of precision and recall on the test split, providing a balanced measure of classification performance.\n\nThe connection between embedding geometry and classification outcomes is evident: the presence of locally isotropic clusters associated with vendors aligns with higher classification accuracy, indicating that the model leverages these geometric structures for decision-making. To further probe model behaviour, we experimented with \"structured input\"\u2014explicitly formatted invoice information designed to aid human readers (e.g., tabular layouts or labeled fields). Contrary to expectations, this structured input did not improve SLM performance, suggesting that the model already captures relevant structure from the raw text.\n\nIn summary, our findings indicate that in-house SLMs like SBERT can deliver high accuracy and generalisability for invoice categorisation, with the added benefits of cost control and data confidentiality. The geometric analysis of embeddings provides insight into model decision-making and highlights the importance of vendor-driven clusters. Future work will focus on scaling this approach to broader financial document types and integrating SLMs into production accounting workflows to further enhance automation and compliance."}}